# Example: fetch hello and try it

File: catalog/examples/try_hello.oot

One full trip around the shelf: search, add, look, run.

## 1. Search

From the opm repo root, run `opm search hello`. Expect one line:
the name `hello`, version `0.1.0`, and a fingerprint. From anywhere
else the disk copy is not found, so run it there or use network.
No line means you are reading an old or empty shelf.

## 2. Add

Run `opm add hello`. Expect a new `hello` folder with two
things inside: `manifest` (the label) and `hello.oo` (the part).
If `opm` refuses, it will say the fingerprint failed. Delete
the folder and fetch again. Never force a refused part.

## 3. Look

Open `manifest`. It says the part's name, version, and what it
is allowed to touch (`caps=FsRead`: it may read files, nothing
more). Open `hello.oo`. The top lines describe what it does.

## 4. Run

Build and run the part with the main controls (`cli run`
on the file). Expect one greeting line and a clean stop.
For what a broken build looks like, see the main controls
guide at `cli/docs/start.oot`.
